  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 136 AM MDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nAs of 1:30AM, we're starting to see some patchy fog develop over \nportions of Larimer and Weld counties, with areas of Laramie\ncounty just to our north down to 1/4 mile visibility. Fog is \nexpected to develop further south in the next few hours, \npotentially impacting the urban corridor, portions of I-25, I-70 \nand I-76. Visibilities down to 1/4 mile are expected for isolated\nareas. A Dense Fog Advisory may be needed if the coverage of the \n1/4 mile visibilities are great enough. The most likely timing of\nfog near the urban corridor will be between 4AM and 8AM.\n\nThe upper level high, that was to our southeast, will start to move \nwest today. This will put us in more zonal flow aloft, which will \nbegin our drying trend. Precipitable water values today will \ndecrease, but will remain around 100% to 125% of normal. A weak \ndisturbance will move through the flow aloft to our south, providing \nthe best lift around the Palmer Divide and south. Surface winds will \nbe more from the southeast today. The SE surface winds will allow \nfor some weak upslope for our northern counties near the Cheyenne \nRidge. With the slightly lower PWs and less widespread lift, \nshowers and storms are expected to be lower in coverage today \ncompared to the past few days. Scattered showers and \nthunderstorms will likely form around the Cheyenne ridge and \nPalmer Divide areas today, moving east/southeast through the \nafternoon and evening. The flash flooding threat will be lower \ntoday, due to the decrease in PWs, decrease in coverage, and \nincrease in storm motions. However, with all the rain we've seen \nlately there could still be some localized flooding concerns due \nto the more saturated soils and lower flash flood guidance. We do\nhave a slight risk for severe weather again this afternoon. Low \nlevel lapse rates will be steep today and 0-6km shear will be \nsufficient for strong to severe storms to develop (~30 to 35 \nkts). But there is a potential bust factor with the severe \nweather threat, due to the possibility of morning fog and low \nclouds sticking around longer than expected and impacting \ninstability. Stronger storms will have the capability of \nproducing large hail, strong winds, and heavy rainfall. \n\nA warming and drying trend is forecast for Monday and Tuesday. Upper \nlevel ridging will start to build to our west as the upper level \nhigh shifts more towards the four corners region. Temperatures on \nMonday will warm closer to normal with much lower rain chances. On \nTuesday, models are still a bit up in the air, but a few of them are \nhinting at a weak cold front Tuesday afternoon and evening moving\nin from the north. The timing of this front will impact highs in \nour northern plains counties for Tuesday and our precip chances \nfor Tuesday. We'll see a brief cooling in the temperatures on \nWednesday behind that front, and an increase in rain chances as we\nsee increased moisture behind the front and maybe some surface \nconvergence from the stalled boundary. But then upper level \nridging really starts to build just to the west of our area for \nThursday and Friday, before shifting east over our area for the \nweekend. Highs are forecast to sit in the mid 80s to mid 90s for \nthe plains for the end of next week.",
